1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:35,000
A welfare-check call arrives during a simulated flood. Should the software send a response unit? In this lab, you will program that decision.

2
00:00:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Search and rescue is the work of finding, reaching, and helping people. The simulation has a hidden answer key, but the controller cannot read it. A call produces visible evidence, TRACE checks the information, and your controller checks the response units before saving an action.

3
00:01:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
TRACE is the system's decision notebook. CLEAR permits a resource check. HOLD stops the controller. Your code then decides whether a suitable unit can actually be sent.

4
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
An allocation selects a unit, a refusal selects none and explains why, and a repair appends a later correction without erasing the earlier decision.

5
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Preflight checks the workshop setup. Continue only after five PASS lines and READY.

6
00:04:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Run one simulated session, then replay it. Byte-identical means every saved file matches, so the same inputs produced the same decisions.

7
00:06:00,000 --> 00:07:35,000
The four cases show allocation, information refusal, capacity refusal, and append-only repair. The medical case proves that CLEAR and allocation are not the same thing.

8
00:07:35,000 --> 00:08:50,000
A capability is a task a unit can perform. Keep only available, reachable, correct-route units with the needed capability, then sort them deterministically.

9
00:08:50,000 --> 00:10:20,000
First reject mismatched calls or situations. Then apply TRACE before capacity. With CLEAR, an empty eligible list refuses; otherwise allocate its first unit.

10
00:10:20,000 --> 00:11:35,000
A repair is a later correction to the record. Keep the earlier allocation and append a new repair only when the update belongs to the same chain.

11
00:11:35,000 --> 00:12:50,000
A failure name points to the rule that still needs work. When every test passes, run your controller and compare it with the completed walkthrough.

12
00:12:50,000 --> 00:13:55,000
The what-if runs change copied resource snapshots. They isolate the resource decision: capacity changes the result while TRACE remains CLEAR.

13
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:50,000
You connected evidence, TRACE, resources, and recorded action. You can now explain both refusals, deterministic allocation, append-only repair, and exact replay. This workshop uses a simulation for learning.
